\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Saved four-state illustration with the generator and retained spaces specified above. (a) Kernel norm for ranks two and three, with rank four identically zero. (b) Operator error from omitting memory, bounded by \(t\|B\|_2\). (c) Residual from trapezoidal evaluation of the exact Volterra equation on the saved grid \(t=0,.025,\ldots  ,10\). This quadrature defect is not a model error. The figure's coupling \(D\) denotes the present \(B\), not the number of categorical fields. (d) For observable \(f=(0,1,0,1)\), discarding within-group coordinates loses a reference-weighted RMS component \(1/2\), while carrying all internal coordinates gives zero loss. The example explains which information must accompany group mass when that observable is needed.}}{13}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dm-four-state}{{5}{13}{Saved four-state illustration with the generator and retained spaces specified above. (a) Kernel norm for ranks two and three, with rank four identically zero. (b) Operator error from omitting memory, bounded by \(t\|B\|_2\). (c) Residual from trapezoidal evaluation of the exact Volterra equation on the saved grid \(t=0,.025,\ldots ,10\). This quadrature defect is not a model error. The figure's coupling \(D\) denotes the present \(B\), not the number of categorical fields. (d) For observable \(f=(0,1,0,1)\), discarding within-group coordinates loses a reference-weighted RMS component \(1/2\), while carrying all internal coordinates gives zero loss. The example explains which information must accompany group mass when that observable is needed}{figure.5}{}}
